    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, Assyria grew from a merchant network into the largest empire the world had yet seen.</li><li>Its provinces, royal couriers, siege engineers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> became models for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and Persia.</li><li>Overstretched and hated by its subjects, it was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> in barely sixty years after its peak.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-nineveh">Library of Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> preserved the standard version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>, one of the oldest surviving works of literature.</p></aside>
    </section>

